For every \(q\in\mathbb N\) and \(L\in\mathbb R\) satisfying \(q\geq 1\) and \(L\geq 4\), there exists a constant \(c_{\mathrm{PI}}=c_{\mathrm{PI}}(q,L)>0\) such that, with \(R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}\) denoting the point-indexed outer-expectation minimax risk in \cref{obj:def:point-indexed-distance-risk},
\[
\liminf_{n\to\infty}
\left(\frac{n}{\log n}\right)^{1/4} R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}
=
\liminf_{n\to\infty}
\frac{R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}}{(\log n/n)^{1/4}},
\]
and
\[
\liminf_{n\to\infty}
\frac{R_n^{\mathrm{PI}}}{(\log n/n)^{1/4}}
\geq c_{\mathrm{PI}}.
\]

9d0a38b301cfbba8592c9767b29e03f27951a946.body:
For a law \(P\), sample size \(n\), degree \(p\), and bandwidth \(h>0\), define the extended nonnegative random variable
\[
\mathrm{GramDev}_{n,p,P}(h)
=
\sup_{t\in\{0,1\}}
\sup_{x\in\mathcal B_P}
\max_{0\leq j,k\leq p}
\left|
\bigl(\widehat\Psi_{t,x}(h)\bigr)_{jk}
-
\bigl(\Psi_{t,P,x}(h)\bigr)_{jk}
\right|.
\]
At the theorem bandwidth \(h_n\), this gives \(\mathrm{GramDev}_{n,p,P}(h_n)\).
